From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:26:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-modeset: make surface registration RM calls without
 nvkms_lock

Registering a DRM framebuffer (NVKMS surface registration) dups the memory
into the NVKMS RM client, queries it, allocates a context DMA and binds it
to the core channel; unregistering frees the context DMA and the dup. All
of it ran under the global nvkms_lock. These RM calls wait for the RM API
lock whenever another RM client holds it (e.g. a CUDA context being created
or destroyed for VA-API decode), measured up to ~4 ms per registration, and
meanwhile the NVKMS kthread (vblank callbacks, flip completion events) and
the compositor's commit thread wait for nvkms_lock even though they don't
call into RM.

For ioctls from kernel-space clients (nvidia-drm through KAPI), which hold
the NVKMS PM lock across the ioctl and keep the device alive across their
calls, drop nvkms_lock around those RM calls:

- Registration reserves the RM handles under the lock, drops it for the
  dup, GET_SURFACE_INFO, context DMA allocation and core channel bind, and
  publishes the surface handle only after retaking the lock, so nothing can
  see the surface half-initialised. If the core channel or all surface
  descriptors were freed in the meantime (new surfaceDescGeneration), the
  descriptors are allocated and bound again with the lock held.
- Unregistration does everything else as before with the lock held and
  hands the context DMA and memory handles to FreeSurfaceRmObjectsUnlocked(),
  which frees them without the lock and releases the handles after
  retaking it.

Only used for the EVO 3 HALs (descriptors are plain context DMAs), without
syncpts or SOC display, with lazy_ctxdma_bind, and for surfaces not imported
by fd; everything else takes the old path.

nvidia_modeset.unlocked_surface_rm=0 (module parameter, writable at
runtime) restores the old behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
